e "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96705/selection/ma-v1/687abf133b8d384f28814f251bb4bb42e4f54ca3032d88b150217e36c63a187d/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "b9e0aabe-c67a-5b63-a7a7-2389382bcc55" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96705/selection/ma-v1/687abf133b8d384f28814f251bb4bb42e4f54ca3032d88b150217e36c63a187d" :answer-field/id #uuid "86a9e1ea-f449-565b-9f12-4d37cf527e40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-96705/selection/ma-v1/687abf133b8d384f28814f251bb4bb42e4f54ca3032d88b150217e36c63a187d/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-96705/selection/ma-v1/687abf133b8d384f28814f251bb4bb42e4f54ca3032d88b150217e36c63a187d/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-96705/selection/ma-v1/687abf133b8d384f28814f251bb4bb42e4f54ca3032d88b150217e36c63a187d/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-96705/selection/ma-v1/687abf133b8d384f28814f251bb4bb42e4f54ca3032d88b150217e36c63a187d/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-96705/selection/ma-v1/687abf133b8d384f28814f251bb4bb42e4f54ca3032d88b150217e36c63a187d/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-96705/selection/ma-v1/687abf133b8d384f28814f251bb4bb42e4f54ca3032d88b150217e36c63a187d/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-96705"] :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=0$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$\n- $5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=0$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/answer-fields ["q-96705/selection/ma-v1/687abf133b8d384f28814f251bb4bb42e4f54ca3032d88b150217e36c63a187d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-118859/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "a0461470-74e1-595b-87a8-6bf2f3b6e07d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118859/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "f6297545-0a2b-5a58-b39b-6e19a58e8907" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118859/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "2a43bcfb-0432-5d5b-b8c6-771e30808d26" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118859/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "1e72bb06-a683-5964-828a-717fa36dc134" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118859/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "69775569-6737-5840-9f64-2fbbe353abc6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118859/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "41e36352-b8f1-5f96-8328-34f73f65a743" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-118859/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-118859/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-118859/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-118859/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-118859/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-118859/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-118859" :question/id #uuid "6fdef7eb-2d32-5a05-8afa-f9593109442c" :question/math-academy-id "q-118859" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-3{x}^{2}=1$\n- ${y}^{′}+6y={e}^{3y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-3{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+1.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+6y & ={e}^{3y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={e}^{3y}-6y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={e}^{3y}-6y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-118859/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118859"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-96387"] :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y}=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′} & =xy+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2(xy+{e}^{x}) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2(xy+{e}^{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-95984"] :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$\n- $2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\sqrt{x}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-96711"] :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-118791"] :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186262093 :question/answer-fields 17592186262094] {:db/id "q-96992/selection/ma-v1/67b63d787185779d054ac23602bc210bcd2ca90e8aed1346bd7d9b84ccd30794/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "974e27e8-0a1f-5427-b0df-adbff40911b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96992/selection/ma-v1/67b63d787185779d054ac23602bc210bcd2ca90e8aed1346bd7d9b84ccd30794/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "50277299-5c67-5275-8268-3fe1a838f0f7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96992/selection/ma-v1/67b63d787185779d054ac23602bc210bcd2ca90e8aed1346bd7d9b84ccd30794/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2f066bb8-15af-5570-a617-a58dad66e744" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96992/selection/ma-v1/67b63d787185779d054ac23602bc210bcd2ca90e8aed1346bd7d9b84ccd30794/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "8cb3c719-1f23-5f4b-8196-fe9eef6f68bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96992/selection/ma-v1/67b63d787185779d054ac23602bc210bcd2ca90e8aed1346bd7d9b84ccd30794/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "63062b70-3bb2-5bec-9722-dc2fdabf4989" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96992/selection/ma-v1/67b63d787185779d054ac23602bc210bcd2ca90e8aed1346bd7d9b84ccd30794" :answer-field/id #uuid "50b77302-cdc7-5688-860d-e49acad9f630" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-96992/selection/ma-v1/67b63d787185779d054ac23602bc210bcd2ca90e8aed1346bd7d9b84ccd30794/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-96992/selection/ma-v1/67b63d787185779d054ac23602bc210bcd2ca90e8aed1346bd7d9b84ccd30794/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-96992/selection/ma-v1/67b63d787185779d054ac23602bc210bcd2ca90e8aed1346bd7d9b84ccd30794/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-96992/selection/ma-v1/67b63d787185779d054ac23602bc210bcd2ca90e8aed1346bd7d9b84ccd30794/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-96992/selection/ma-v1/67b63d787185779d054ac23602bc210bcd2ca90e8aed1346bd7d9b84ccd30794/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-96992/selection/ma-v1/67b63d787185779d054ac23602bc210bcd2ca90e8aed1346bd7d9b84ccd30794/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-96992"] :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\n- $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/answer-fields ["q-96992/selection/ma-v1/67b63d787185779d054ac23602bc210bcd2ca90e8aed1346bd7d9b84ccd30794"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-118788/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "819837bc-3c26-5982-be1c-aba248d960bf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118788/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "0847046b-ab2c-5c03-9d24-c78203613c35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118788/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "ff03baea-fc93-5382-974b-f89e934de061" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118788/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "cb518d2b-76e8-5383-9317-12e231c1d909" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118788/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "7aff5497-9742-5c3b-ba29-c7220a0e52c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118788/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "023a2699-f925-5c39-8ed3-1368ca4c51ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-118788/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-118788/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-118788/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-118788/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" "q-118788/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-118788/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-118788" :question/id #uuid "e81dead2-8026-5bbf-8274-50b6fe156c6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118788" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $6{y}^{″}+xy={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-118788/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118788"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8460"] :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8461"] :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
